WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.461
The last page laid out the architecture.

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:04.921
Scheduler.

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:06.382
Lock manager.

00:00:06.382 --> 00:00:07.843
WAL.

00:00:07.843 --> 00:00:09.304
Recovery manager.

00:00:09.304 --> 00:00:10.764
Now we run them.

00:00:10.764 --> 00:00:12.225
Three transactions at once.

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:13.686
T one.

00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:15.147
T two.

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:16.607
T three.

00:00:16.607 --> 00:00:18.068
Watch the whole machine move together as one.

00:00:18.068 --> 00:00:21.502
Three transactions, three variables.

00:00:21.502 --> 00:00:24.936
T one and T two both touch counter A.

00:00:24.936 --> 00:00:28.369
T three touches B, then C.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:31.803
They share the lock manager and the WAL, and that shared machinery is where coordination has to happen.

00:00:31.803 --> 00:00:35.237
Strict two-phase locking keeps it safe.

00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:38.671
Each transaction holds every lock until it ends, so no one reads another's uncommitted data.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:42.105
Now read the schedule with me, one step at a time.

00:00:42.105 --> 00:00:45.539
The counters start at A nineteen, B thirty-two, C twenty-seven.

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:48.972
T two reeds A.

00:00:48.972 --> 00:00:52.406
T two writes A up to twenty-two point eight.

00:00:52.406 --> 00:00:55.840
T three reeds B.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.274
T one reeds A.

00:00:59.274 --> 00:01:02.708
T three writes B to thirty-eight point four.

00:01:02.708 --> 00:01:06.142
T one writes A to twenty-seven point three six.

00:01:06.142 --> 00:01:09.575
T three reeds C.

00:01:09.575 --> 00:01:13.009
And T three writes C to thirty-two point four.

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:16.443
Eight steps, three transactions, all interleaved.

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:19.265
Scenario one.

00:01:19.265 --> 00:01:22.087
All three commit cleanly.

00:01:22.087 --> 00:01:24.909
Watch the WAL.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:27.731
T two writes A, and that change goes to the log first.

00:01:27.731 --> 00:01:30.552
Then T two commits.

00:01:30.552 --> 00:01:33.374
Once that commit record reaches the WAL, T two's change is durable, and only then do its locks come off.

00:01:33.374 --> 00:01:36.196
T three writes B.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:39.018
T one writes A.

00:01:39.018 --> 00:01:41.840
T one commits.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.662
T three writes C.

00:01:44.662 --> 00:01:47.484
T three commits.

00:01:47.484 --> 00:01:50.306
Every change was logged before its commit, so each commit makes its change durable on disk.

00:01:50.306 --> 00:01:53.127
Three transactions advance the database to one new consistent state.

00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:55.949
A twenty-seven point three six.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.771
B thirty-eight point four.

00:01:58.771 --> 00:02:01.593
C thirty-two point four.

00:02:01.593 --> 00:02:04.415
That is durability, all three writes guaranteed to survive a crash.

00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:07.039
Scenario two is the interesting one.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:09.663
Here T three aborts partway through.

00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:12.288
So do T one and T two have to roll back too?

00:02:12.288 --> 00:02:14.912
No.

00:02:14.912 --> 00:02:17.536
Only T three unwinds.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:20.160
Watch the log.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.784
T two writes A.

00:02:22.784 --> 00:02:25.409
T three writes B.

00:02:25.409 --> 00:02:28.033
T two commits.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.657
T three writes C.

00:02:30.657 --> 00:02:33.281
T one writes A.

00:02:33.281 --> 00:02:35.905
Now T three fails.

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:38.530
The recovery manager reeds T three's WAL entries backward.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.154
It undoes its write on C, restoring twenty-seven.

00:02:41.154 --> 00:02:43.778
It undoes its write on B, restoring thirty-two.

00:02:43.778 --> 00:02:46.402
Then T three aborts, leaving no trace.

00:02:46.402 --> 00:02:49.026
That is atomicity.

00:02:49.026 --> 00:02:51.651
All of T three, or none of it.

00:02:51.651 --> 00:02:54.275
And T one still commits normally.

00:02:54.275 --> 00:02:56.899
The final state is A twenty-seven point three six, B thirty-two, C twenty-seven.

00:02:56.899 --> 00:03:00.767
Here is the strict in strict two-phase locking earning its name.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:04.634
T three held its exclusive locks on B and C the whole time.

00:03:04.634 --> 00:03:08.502
It never released them before aborting.

00:03:08.502 --> 00:03:12.370
So no other transaction could read B or C while T three had them dirty.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.238
No one read uncommitted data, so no one inherits T three's failure.

00:03:16.238 --> 00:03:20.105
One abort unwinds one transaction.

00:03:20.105 --> 00:03:23.973
It does not cascade across the system.

00:03:23.973 --> 00:03:26.509
That trace is what a database does on every commit.

00:03:26.509 --> 00:03:29.044
Locks hold the order.

00:03:29.044 --> 00:03:31.580
The WAL makes every change durable or reversible.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.115
Here nothing broke.

00:03:34.115 --> 00:03:36.651
Next, something does.
